Explain the differences between var, let, and const in JavaScript regarding variable scope, hoisting, and reassignment.
ans.
  var is a function-scoped variable, and when it is declared inside a function, it will only be available within that function.
    code example;
    function greetUser() {
      var message ="hello masha!";
      console.log(message);
    }
      
  let and const are block-scoped; they exist inside the exact set of curly braces{} where they're already written.
    code example;
      let status = "logged out";
       if (true) {
         let status = "logged in"
         console.log (status);
       }

  var is hoisted with a default value of "undefined"=during compilation phase; all declarations move to the top of their scope.
    It automatically initializes the default value of undefined.
    code example;
    var username = "najma"
    console.log(username);

 let is hoisted but uninitialized(temporal dead zone) =when entering a let declaration in a block, 
  JavaScript registers the variable name in memory, unlike var.
   where it doesn't attach any value to it.
      code example;
       console.log(role);
         let role = "admin";
          console.log(role);
 const is hoisted but uninitialised(temporal dead zone) =when a block containing a const is declared,
 the name is registered in memory. Leaving it completely uninitialised!
  code example;
    console.log(maxUsers);
     const maxUsers = 100;
    console.log (maxUsers);

 var and let are allowed to reassign because their values tend to change over time,
   whereas const is designed to lock a variable binding so that it can never point to a new memory address or a new value!
   code example;
     const user = { name: "Alice" };
       user.name ="Bob";
       user = { name: "Sahara" };

